Issues.Body:
In `ngtsc`'s `NgModuleDecoratorHandler.analyze`, when `declarations` contains an external `.d.ts` class (`NG6001`) or `resolveTypeList` fails (`NG1010`/`NG6005`), `analyze` returns `{diagnostics}` without `analysis` (or throws `FatalDiagnosticError`). Consequently, `TraitCompiler` keeps the trait in `TraitState.Analyzed` with `analysis === null`, leaves the `@NgModule(...)` decorator on the class untouched, and omits `ɵmod`, `ɵinj`, `ɵfac`, and `ɵsetClassMetadata`.

In `ng-analyze`, `optimize_ng_module` previously cleared `declarations`/`imports`/`exports` when `outcome.failed` was `true`, which still emitted an empty `NgModuleMetadata` over the wire in full compilation mode — causing `processor.ts` to strip `@NgModule(...)` and emit an empty `ɵfac`/`ɵmod`/`ɵinj`/`ɵsetClassMetadata`.

This change:
1. Adds `analysis_failed: bool` to `NgModuleData` and sets it to `true` in `optimize_ng_module` when `outcome.failed` is `true`.
2. Suppresses `NgModuleMetadata` in `ClassData::to_wire` and filters pure failed `@NgModule` classes out of `FileData::to_wire` in full compilation mode, while preserving `scope_diagnostics` and any co-located `@Injectable`/`@Service` metadata.
3. Skips downstream `NG6100` (`module.id`) and `NG2003` constructor dependency diagnostics on failed `@NgModule` classes to match `ngtsc`.

### Input

```ts
// ext.d.ts
import * as i0 from '@angular/core';
export declare class ExternalComponent {
  static ɵcmp: i0.ɵɵComponentDeclaration<
    ExternalComponent,
    'ext-comp',
    never,
    {},
    {},
    never,
    never,
    false,
    never
  >;
}

// app.ts
import {NgModule} from '@angular/core';
import {ExternalComponent} from './ext';

@NgModule({
  declarations: [ExternalComponent],
})
export class BrokenModule {}
```

### Output Before Fix (`ngp`)

Reports `NG6001`, strips `@NgModule`, and emits empty definitions:
```ts
export class BrokenModule {
  static ɵfac = function BrokenModule_Factory(__ngFactoryType__: any): BrokenModule {
    return new (__ngFactoryType__ || BrokenModule)();
  };
  static ɵmod = /*@__PURE__*/ i0.ɵɵdefineNgModule({type: BrokenModule});
  static ɵinj = /*@__PURE__*/ i0.ɵɵdefineInjector({});
}
```

### Output After Fix (`ngp` — matches `ngtsc`)

Reports `NG6001` and leaves `BrokenModule` uncompiled without stripping `@NgModule` or emitting `ɵfac`/`ɵmod`/`ɵinj`:
```ts
@NgModule({
  declarations: [ExternalComponent],
})
export class BrokenModule {}
```

In Google/Closure TypeScript compilations, `closure.lib.d.ts` declares `declare var COMPILED: boolean;`. In `ngtsc`'s `PartialEvaluator`, an unbound `declare var` with a non-literal type (`boolean`) resolves via `visitVariableDeclaration` to a variable `Reference(COMPILED)`, which is truthy in JavaScript boolean expressions (e.g. `COMPILED && USE_MOCK_DATA` evaluates to `USE_MOCK_DATA`).

Because `ng-analyze` performs single-file lexical resolution without loading ambient `closure.lib.d.ts`, `Interpreter::visit_reference` saw `reference.symbol_id() == None` for `COMPILED` and returned `DynamicReason::UnknownIdentifier("COMPILED")`.

This change updates `Interpreter::visit_reference` so that when `reference.symbol_id()` is `None` (ensuring any local declaration shadows it) and `name == "COMPILED"`, it returns `ResolvedValue::Reference(self.make_reference(name.to_string(), None, span, DeclKind::Variable))`.

### Input

```ts
import {Component} from '@angular/core';

const SELECTOR = COMPILED && 'app-compiled';

@Component({
  selector: SELECTOR || 'app-fallback',
  template: '',
})
export class AppComponent {}
```

### Evaluation Before Fix (`ngp`)

- `COMPILED` evaluated to `Dynamic(UnknownIdentifier("COMPILED"))`, failing static evaluation of `SELECTOR` (`NG1010: Value could not be determined statically`).

### Evaluation After Fix (`ngp` — matches `ngtsc` with `closure.lib.d.ts`)

- `COMPILED` resolves to `Reference(COMPILED, DeclKind::Variable)` (when not shadowed by a local binding), allowing `COMPILED && 'app-compiled'` to evaluate to `'app-compiled'`.

In `ngtsc`'s `PartialEvaluator`, `foreignFunctionResolver` receives `resolveFfrExpr` (`visitFfrExpression`), which sets `res.synthetic = true` on any returned `Reference`. Consequently, `createModuleWithProvidersResolver` marks the unwrapped `ngModule` reference as `synthetic = true`, causing `NgModuleDecoratorHandler` to set `remoteScopesMayRequireCycleProtection = true` (`(() => { ... })()` wrapper) and preventing duplicate unwrapped `.ngModule` entries in `ɵinj.imports`.

In `ng-analyze`, when a function or static method call (such as `FeatureModule.forRoot()`) could not be statically evaluated from its body and fell back to `try_foreign_function_call` using the return type `ModuleWithProviders<T>`, `resolve_type` did not call `mark_value_synthetic(...)` and `mark_value_synthetic` lacked an arm for `ResolvedValue::Synthetic(SyntheticValue::ModuleWithProviders { .. })`. Because `ng_module.synthetic` remained `false`:
1. `ɵinj.imports` emitted both the raw call expression and the unwrapped `T` module reference (`[FeatureModule.forRoot(), i1.FeatureModule]`).
2. `remote_scopes_may_require_cycle_protection` was not set to `true`.

This change wraps `self.resolve_type_name(name, &Scope::new())` in `mark_value_synthetic(...)` inside `try_foreign_function_call` and adds an arm for `ResolvedValue::Synthetic(SyntheticValue::ModuleWithProviders { .. })` in `mark_value_synthetic`.

### Input

```ts
// feature.d.ts
import * as i0 from '@angular/core';
import {ModuleWithProviders} from '@angular/core';
export declare class FeatureModule {
  static forRoot(): ModuleWithProviders<FeatureModule>;
  static ɵmod: i0.ɵɵNgModuleDeclaration<FeatureModule, never, never, never>;
}

// app.ts
import {NgModule} from '@angular/core';
import {FeatureModule} from './feature';

@NgModule({
  imports: [FeatureModule.forRoot()],
})
export class AppModule {}
```

### Output Before Fix (`ngp`)

```ts
export class AppModule {
  static ɵfac = function AppModule_Factory(__ngFactoryType__: any): AppModule {
    return new (__ngFactoryType__ || AppModule)();
  };
  static ɵmod = /*@__PURE__*/ i0.ɵɵdefineNgModule({type: AppModule});
  static ɵinj = /*@__PURE__*/ i0.ɵɵdefineInjector({
    imports: [FeatureModule.forRoot(), i1.FeatureModule],
  });
}
```

### Output After Fix (`ngp` — matches `ngtsc`)

```ts
export class AppModule {
  static ɵfac = function AppModule_Factory(__ngFactoryType__: any): AppModule {
    return new (__ngFactoryType__ || AppModule)();
  };
  static ɵmod = /*@__PURE__*/ i0.ɵɵdefineNgModule({type: AppModule});
  static ɵinj = /*@__PURE__*/ i0.ɵɵdefineInjector({
    imports: [FeatureModule.forRoot()],
  });
}
```

In `ngtsc`'s `downlevelDecoratorsTransform`, `classParameters` is `[]` (non-null) whenever an explicit constructor declaration with a body exists on a class, and `null` only when no constructor declaration exists. When `hasAngularDecorator` is `true` and `classParameters !== null`, `ngtsc` emits `static ctorParameters = () => [];` — which is also necessary in JIT inheritance so a subclass declaring `constructor() { super(...); }` does not inherit its base class's `ctorParameters`.

In `ng-analyze`, `constructorParams` is `undefined` when a class has no constructor and `[]` when a class declares an explicit zero-argument `constructor() {}`. However, `processFileSync` previously checked `if (constructorParams && constructorParams.length > 0)` in the `isJit` block and omitted `static ctorParameters` for explicit zero-argument constructors. This change removes `&& constructorParams.length > 0` so `static ctorParameters = (): any => [];` is emitted for explicit zero-argument constructors while continuing to omit `ctorParameters` when no constructor is declared.

### Input

```ts
import {Component, Directive} from '@angular/core';

@Directive({
  selector: '[empty-ctor-dir]',
  jit: true,
})
export class EmptyCtorDir {
  constructor() {}
}

@Component({
  selector: 'no-ctor-cmp',
  template: '',
  jit: true,
})
export class NoCtorCmp {}
```

### Output Before Fix (`ngp`)

