### Description

`TreeItem` and `TreeItemGroup` throw NG0950 when a component containing them is destroyed synchronously after `TestBed.createComponent()`, before its first change detection.

The required inputs are bound in the template. However, `ngOnInit` has not run and the input bindings have not been applied, while `ngOnDestroy` still reads them when attempting to unregister.

This affects early teardown, for example a unit test that creates a component and destroys it without running change detection. I reproduced this in the repository Chromium test runner. I have not verified how frequently it occurs in production applications; ordinary initialized trees are not affected by this reproduction.

### Reproduction

The minimal failing operation, using either fixture defined below, is:

```ts
const fixture = TestBed.createComponent(StaticFlatTree);
fixture.destroy(); // NG0950: required input "parent" has not been set yet.
```

With `StaticNestedTree`, the same operation reports the required `ownedBy` input.

This is a synchronous creation/teardown lifecycle reproduction rather than a visible interaction, so I am including complete TestBed fixtures and the exact repository test command instead of an interactive StackBlitz.

#### Repository setup

1. Check out angular/components at `e59fce84b`.
2. Install the Node.js version in `.nvmrc` and pnpm from `package.json#packageManager`, then run `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`.
3. In `src/aria/tree/tree.spec.ts`, add the following fixtures and tests as a separate top-level `describe`, outside the existing `describe('Tree')` (its `afterEach` expects its own initialized fixture). The imports listed below are already present; reuse them instead of adding duplicate imports.
4. Run `pnpm test src/aria/tree --no-watch`.

```ts
// Already imported in tree.spec.ts:
// import {Component, ChangeDetectionStrategy} from "@angular/core";
// import {TestBed} from "@angular/core/testing";
// import {Tree} from "./tree";
// import {TreeItem} from "./tree-item";
// import {TreeItemGroup} from "./tree-item-group";

@Component({
  template: `
    <ul ngTree #tree="ngTree">
      <li ngTreeItem [parent]="tree" value="item">Item</li>
    </ul>
  `,
  imports: [Tree, TreeItem],
  changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.Eager,
})
class StaticFlatTree {}

@Component({
  template: `
    <ul ngTree #tree="ngTree">
      <li ngTreeItem [parent]="tree" value="parent" #parentItem="ngTreeItem">
        Parent
        <ul role="group">
          <ng-template ngTreeItemGroup [ownedBy]="parentItem" #group="ngTreeItemGroup">
            <li ngTreeItem [parent]="group" value="child">Child</li>
          </ng-template>
        </ul>
      </li>
    </ul>
  `,
  imports: [Tree, TreeItem, TreeItemGroup],
  changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.Eager,
})
class StaticNestedTree {}

describe("Tree destruction", () => {
  it("destroys a flat tree before first change detection", () => {
    const fixture = TestBed.createComponent(StaticFlatTree);
    expect(() => fixture.destroy()).not.toThrow();
  });

  it("destroys a nested tree before first change detection", () => {
    const fixture = TestBed.createComponent(StaticNestedTree);
    expect(() => fixture.destroy()).not.toThrow();
  });

  it("destroys an initialized flat tree (control)", async () => {
    const fixture = TestBed.createComponent(StaticFlatTree);
    await fixture.whenStable();
    expect(() => fixture.destroy()).not.toThrow();
  });

  it("destroys an initialized nested tree (control)", async () => {
    const fixture = TestBed.createComponent(StaticNestedTree);
    await fixture.whenStable();
    expect(() => fixture.destroy()).not.toThrow();
  });
});
```

Do not call `fixture.detectChanges()` or await `fixture.whenStable()` before `destroy()` in the first two tests: that initializes the bindings and bypasses the reproduction. Use static template elements; putting the items inside `@if`/`@for` changes when they are created.

The `ngTreeItemGroup` directive itself is created with the static template, even though its child item inside `ng-template` is deferred.

### Expected Behavior

Destroying before initialization should complete without throwing. Nothing was registered in `ngOnInit`, so there should be nothing to unregister. Destruction after initialization should continue to unregister normally.

### Actual Behavior

The flat-tree test throws:

```text
NG0950: Input "parent" is required but no value is available yet.
```

The nested-tree test throws:

```text
NG0950: Input "ownedBy" is required but no value is available yet.
```

With the four tests in the reproduction, the Chromium suite reports **144 passed, 2 failed**. Only the two pre-initialization tests fail; both initialized control tests pass.

#### Relevant cleanup code

`TreeItem.ngOnDestroy()` reads `parent()` and `tree()`, and `TreeItemGroup.ngOnDestroy()` reads `ownedBy()`. Registration happens in `ngOnInit`.

- [TreeItem at the tested revision](https://github.com/angular/components/blob/e59fce84b/src/aria/tree/tree-item.ts)
- [TreeItemGroup at the tested revision](https://github.com/angular/components/blob/e59fce84b/src/aria/tree/tree-item-group.ts)

#### Related issue and PR

This appears to be the same lifecycle issue as #33972, fixed for combobox in #33974. A corresponding tree fix can retain the references used during registration and only unregister them if initialization registered them. The implementation and regression tests are in #34059.

This report covers `@angular/aria/tree` only.

